Ask a general manager how standards are followed across the hotel and you will usually hear seven different answers. Front office has a binder at the desk. Housekeeping has a checklist and a supervisor walking floors. F&B has a WhatsApp group. The kitchen has a clipboard on the walk-in door. Engineering has a job book. Security has a round sheet. The spa has whatever the spa manager set up.

Each of those systems can work for its own department. None of them lets anyone see the whole property. That is the case for one **SOP app for hotels**: not a different tool per department, but one way of running every department's standards, writing to one record.

## Why does every hotel department need the same SOP app?

Because guests experience the hotel as one service, not seven departments. An arrival depends on the front desk, a ready room, a working AC and a clean restaurant the next morning. When each department proves its standards differently, a failure between departments has no owner and no evidence. One SOP app gives every department the same way to run and prove its work.

## What a departmental SOP looks like in practice

A [departmental SOP](/glossary/departmental-sop) is a standard owned by one department for a repeatable task. Every hotel department has dozens. Here is one per department, the way they run in Mise:

| Department | Standard | Timed task | Photo gate |
|---|---|---|---|
| Front office | FO-204 Arrival to key | VIP arrival, 6-minute target | ID and room-ready confirmation |
| Housekeeping | HSK-101 Guest room reset | Room 208, 26-minute target | Bathroom finish |
| F&B service | FB-310 Restaurant reset | Breakfast close, 18-minute target | Table setup |
| Kitchen | KIT-115 Cold-chain check | Walk-in temperature log, every 4 hours | Thermometer reading |
| Engineering | ENG-402 Fault first response | AC complaint in room 512, 15-minute target | Fixed and tested |
| Security and safety | SEC-020 Fire-exit round | Night round, floors 1 to 14 | Each exit clear |
| Spa and wellness | SPA-210 Treatment room turnover | 12-minute target | Room reset |

The content of each standard is completely different. The mechanism is not. Every one of them is a [timed task](/glossary/timed-task) on the phone of the person doing the work, with steps on screen, a target time, photo evidence at the steps that matter and a supervisor's sign-off.

## What changes when every department runs the same way

### The general manager sees one picture

When every department writes to the same [service record](/glossary/service-record), the question "how is the hotel doing this morning?" has one answer. The manager dashboard shows what is closed, in progress, blocked or late across front office, housekeeping, F&B, kitchen, engineering, security and spa, with exceptions first.

### Handoffs between departments get evidence

Many hotel failures happen between departments. A room is released by housekeeping but the AC fault engineering logged is still open. A VIP arrives before the room-ready confirmation reaches the desk. When both sides of a handoff run in the same app, the evidence is already there: the room was released at 08:42, the fault was fixed and tested at 09:27, the arrival closed at 09:31.

### Supervisors check exceptions, not everything

In every department the supervisor bottleneck looks the same: one person who has to see everything before it counts. Photo evidence inside each task lets the duty manager, sous chef, chief engineer or security manager review exceptions from a dashboard and inspect in person only where it matters.

### Audits stop being a department-by-department scramble

A brand audit, an insurer's visit or a hygiene inspection rarely stays in one department. With one record, the evidence for the fire-exit rounds, the kitchen logs and the room standards is retrieved the same way: filter by standard, date and area, and export.

## Hotel department SOP software vs one tool per department

There are good specialised tools for some departments: maintenance systems for assets, kitchen systems for recipes and costing. The point of one **hotel SOP app** is not to replace those. It is to run the *standards* every department follows, and to prove them, in one place.

- **One staff app.** Front desk agents, attendants, servers, cooks, technicians, officers and therapists all use the same phone app, so a person moving between departments does not learn a new tool.
- **One standards workspace.** Every department head writes SOPs the same way: steps, target time, reference photos, photo gates.
- **One record.** Every completed task, in every department, writes an attributable entry: standard and version, person, place, times against target, photos and sign-off.

This is what hotel department SOP software should mean: the same discipline in every department, not the same content.

## How to roll it out across departments

1. **Start where standards slip most.** Pick one property and the department where missed standards hurt most today. Convert its three to five most important SOPs into timed tasks.
2. **Let the record fill.** Run real shifts for the first weeks. The service record shows what is done on time, what is late and where evidence is missing.
3. **Add the neighbouring department.** Extend to the department on the other side of your most painful handoff: housekeeping and engineering, front office and housekeeping, F&B and kitchen.
4. **Extend property by property.** Groups publish the standards once and run them at each property, adapting only what genuinely differs.

For the step-by-step conversion of a single SOP, see how to [digitize hotel SOPs](/digital-sop).

## What one SOP app for hotels does not do

It is worth being precise. An SOP app for hotels does not replace your PMS, your maintenance asset register or your recipe costing. It does not certify compliance with any regulation. It runs your standards and records evidence that they were followed, in every department, every shift.

## The short version

Seven departments, seven ways of keeping standards, and no way to see the whole hotel: that is the default. One SOP app for hotels keeps each department's standards its own, runs them all the same way, and gives the property one audit-ready service record. See [solutions by department](/solutions) for how each department runs in Mise.
